{
  "_note": "Community tier lists, transcribed verbatim from mythag.net. Two SEPARATE rankings: how well a character carries as main DPS, and how much they improve a team as support. Keeping them apart is the whole point — Kathigu-Ra is A as a DPS and C as a support, Clementine is the reverse, and the single `tier` field on annotations/awakeners.json cannot express either. `dpsFloor` / `supportFloor` record the investment level the source printed in parentheses next to a portrait, e.g. \"Mouchette (E2)\"; that is the level at which the rank applies, and it is role-specific, so a unit can be listed at different floors on the two lists (Murphy: Fauxborn is E2 as a DPS and unqualified as a support). Absent from a list means the source did not rank them there at all — per its own note, you would need a crazy reason to use them in that role. That is meaningful data, so absence is left as absence rather than backfilled with a low grade.",
  "_source": "mythag.net/handbook/tier-list and /handbook/tier-list-endgame, transcribed 2026-09-08 (after the 5-9 Sep D-Effect Zone rework). D-Effect Zone difficulties are Normal / Hard / Nightmare / Madness; the retired Alert 1-5 system covered only Normal (1-3) and Hard (4-5).",
  "_dpsScale": {
    "S": "Newbie solo carry - very little investment needed to clear Normal and Hard stages.",
    "A": "Great in Hard - clears Normal and Hard stages as main DPS even with a suboptimal team.",
    "B+": "Great in Normal - clears Normal stages as main DPS, but needs specific teammates or high investment for Hard.",
    "B": "Viable with support - clears Normal stages as main DPS, but needs specific teammates or high investment to work well.",
    "C": "Not recommended - an unrealistic amount of effort for a new player; better used later as support or secondary DPS."
  },
  "_supportScale": {
    "S": "Meta defining - extremely powerful support, good in many different teams.",
    "A": "Game changing - a premium support that lets otherwise weak teams punch above their weight.",
    "B": "Role player - good at one or two specific things, valuable when you need them.",
    "C+": "Niche role - a unique ability that no other character has, valuable in rare situations.",
    "C": "Technically usable - works as a support, but others fill the same role better."
  },
  "_endgameNote": "The endgame lists are a SEPARATE ranking for D-Effect Zone Nightmare and Madness. They assume months of play, money spent, and every dupe and SSR wheel a team needs, so they diverge sharply from the newbie lists - Clementine is newbie C as a DPS and endgame A, GHelot is newbie A and endgame C+ support. Never substitute one for the other. The source calls these provisional: the new difficulties had only just released.",
  "_endgameDpsScale": {
    "A": "High Madness viable - Max HP % damage or very high scaling; consistently kills high Madness bosses.",
    "B": "Low Madness viable - reaches the ~10 million damage ceiling for high Nightmare and low Madness.",
    "C": "Nightmare viable - reaches the ~5 million damage ceiling for high Hard and low Nightmare.",
    "X": "Secondary DPS - contributes meaningful damage alongside a main carry, usually via a Max HP % nuke."
  },
  "_endgameSupportScale": {
    "S": "Meta defining - huge damage multipliers that lift lower-tier DPS into Nightmare and Madness viability.",
    "A": "Game changing - defensive tools, extra turns, or Max HP % damage that high-difficulty stages need.",
    "B": "Role player - useful enough in a specific team to run over a more universally useful support.",
    "C": "Niche role - not useful in most high-difficulty stages, but matters for specific fights."
  },
